at a community center in southeast  London they're getting back into  training what makes this football club  different is it's the only one in the  country specifically for Muslim women  Sisterhood FC only started in 2018 but  it's clear how much it means to its  members I came across them through  Instagram  Sisterhood FC and I saw that there were  lots of hijabis lots of girls who looked  like me and I thought let me let me give  it a shot and I went to their training  session and it was so much fun and all  the girls were so welcoming and lovely  and I never went back to anything else  initially the club struggled with  funding but recently for the first time  it's played five aside and seven aside  in the ladies Super League  we were really determined to say like  well yeah this is what we look like but  we could still play war and we can still  get pretty far in the league and we came  forth and it was for our first kind of  League I think we're really happy about  that to be fair regardless of results  it's already proving invaluable for  young women from across the capital  recently some have got together to watch  World Cup matches it's nice to see that  the World Cup is for everyone and it's  like it can be represented in countries  outside of Europe and in like Muslim  countries so it's it's nice to see that  and  um I was I was supporting Iran when they  were playing with they're out with the  league now sort of like I've lost  interest a little bit well we might be a  long way from the World Cup stadiums in  Qatar but for these players what matters  most is simply enjoying the sport and  being able to be themselves  and as it attracts new players the hope  is the club can offer a welcoming space  to more Muslim women for years to come  Nadine barber Al Jazeera rotherhine in  London
 

